# Merge behaviour for generated files that carry no context (SHIP-15c).
#
# `merge=union` takes both sides of a conflicting hunk instead of writing conflict markers.
# That is wrong for almost every file — it silently interleaves two people's code — so it is
# applied here to exactly one, for a reason specific to it.
#
# routes_golden.txt is the rendered list of every endpoint the service serves, one route per
# line, generated by a test. Three properties together make union the right merge:
#
#   1. Every line is independent. There is no syntax to break and no ordering that has to
#      hold for the file to parse, because nothing parses it — a test regenerates it and
#      compares.
#   2. A conflict in it looks trivially resolvable and is not. Three adjacent one-line
#      additions produce a conflict whose tempting resolution is to keep one side, and the
#      cost of that is a dropped route: no compile error, no failing test in the domain that
#      lost it, and no symptom until somebody calls an endpoint that quietly stopped
#      existing. That is the single most likely way parallel work breaks this service
#      (Docs/10 §4.1).
#   3. A union is always a superset, and a superset is always caught. The merged file has
#      both sides' routes but in whatever order the union produced, which fails
#      TestRouteTableMatchesGolden — and that failure prints the regenerate command.
#
# So the conflict becomes a test failure with instructions attached, and the failure mode is
# "regenerate this" rather than "an endpoint is missing and nobody knows".
services/core/cmd/api/routes_golden.txt merge=union

# Deliberately NOT applied to contracts/openapi.yaml, and the difference is worth stating.
#
# A union-merged YAML document is either invalid or, worse, valid and subtly wrong — two
# `paths:` keys interleaved, a `$ref` orphaned from the entry it belongs to. A YAML conflict
# is ugly and obvious; a union-merged one is neither, and it is the harder of the two to
# notice. The resolution recipe for that file is written above its `paths:` block, and
# TestEveryFragmentIsReferenced catches the half a person would miss.
